timestamp=2026-11-09T08:24:31.179Z level=INFO run=62a397e7 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="cat > /tmp/identify_outliers_final.py << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/synthetic_stroke_data.csv')\n\n# Step 1: Discard values that cannot occur (thresholds)\n# avg_glucose_level: 20-800; bmi: 10-60; age not exceeding 120 (so age > 120 is invalid)\ndf_clean = df.copy()\n# Keep only within realistic thresholds\nmask_real = ((df_clean['avg_glucose_level'] >= 20) & (df_clean['avg_glucose_level'] <= 800) &\n             (df_clean['bmi'] >= 10) & (df_clean['bmi'] <= 60) &\n             (df_clean['age'] <= 120))\ndf_clean = df_clean[mask_real].copy()\n\n# Track outlier indices (original indices) to be removed/final outliers?\n# The task says: \"Remove standard outliers from the data...\" and later identifies other outliers.\n# But at the end: \"Return list of all outliers indexes.\" (all outliers identified across steps)\n# Also note: \"Outlier indices should be mapped back to the original matrix using a unique variable. Note that first row of the dataset is indexed as 0.\"\n# So final answer is indices in the original dataframe (index in original CSV as loaded).\n# But we filtered rows - their original indices remain intact in pandas if we don't reset. Good.\n\n# Step 2: 3*IQR outlier detection\noutliers_standard = set()\nfor col in ['age', 'bmi', 'avg_glucose_level']:\n    Q1 = df_clean[col].quantile(0.25)\n    Q3 = df_clean[col].quantile(0.75)\n    IQR = Q3 - Q1\n    lower = Q1 - 3 * IQR\n    upper = Q3 + 3 * IQR\n    m = (df_clean[col] < lower) | (df_clean[col] > upper)\n    outliers_standard.update(df_clean[m].index.tolist())\n\n# Step 3: Special handling for avg_glucose_level outliers (check stroke association)\n# For observations in avg_glucose_level identified as outliers by IQR (within df_clean scope)\n# but also need to consider the rule as stated. Let's reidentify glucose outliers and apply rules.\ncol = 'avg_glucose_level'\nQ1 = df_clean[col].quantile(0.25)\nQ3 = df_clean[col].quantile(0.75)\nIQR = Q3 - Q1\nlower = Q1 - 3 * IQR\nupper = Q3 + 3 * IQR\ngluc_out_mask = (df_clean[col] < lower) | (df_clean[col] > upper)\ngluc_outliers_idx = set(df_clean[gluc_out_mask].index.tolist())\n\n# Apply the special rule\nnot_outlier_due_to_stroke = set()\nfor idx, row in df_clean[gluc_out_mask].iterrows():\n    v = row[col]\n    # look at other observations sharing the same values (in df_clean, same v)\n    others = df_clean[df_clean[col] == v]\n    total = len(others)\n    stroke = (others['stroke'] == 1).sum()\n    pct = stroke / total * 100 if total > 0 else 0\n    if pct >= 50:\n        # not considered outliers\n        not_outlier_due_to_stroke.add(idx)\n    elif pct > 20:\n        # apply base-2 log transform and recheck - as per rule\n        # recheck: \"the above check should be performed again\" (50%+ rule)\n        # transform values for the purpose of this check? Or transform the data?\n        others_log = others.copy()\n        others_log[col] = np.log2(others_log[col])\n        # check pct of stroke after transform? Or check the condition on transformed scale's group?\n        # The rule is a bit ambiguous. But most plausible: after log transform, do we still consider them outliers\n        # under the same stroke association criterion? Or does the transformation affect whether they remain \"outliers\"?\n        # Usually, the rule is: if many strokes occur at that extreme value, it's clinically relevant, not noise.\n        # After log transform, we re-evaluate the \"50% or more\" condition on the same grouped observations (transformed).\n        # If still >=50% stroke cases dominate at that transformed-space value? Or just recheck the same logic.\n        # Let's see: maybe after transform, the distribution changes. But the grouping is by \"same values\" - values become different after transform.\n        # That means after log2, each observation's value changes; so \"sharing the same values\" group is different. That might split them.\n        # But this is the only interpretation that makes sense. Let us check the actual data pattern.\n        v_log = np.log2(v)\n        others_log_same = others_log[np.isclose(others_log[col], v_log)]\n        total_log = len(others_log)\n        # Or simpler: group by transformed value across all? Or just check if still meets >=50% at that value's log?\n        # Alternative interpretation: reapply the IQR (or outlier detection) on log-transformed avg_glucose_level,\n        # and see if these specific observations are still outliers. That’s plausible.\n        pass\n\nprint('Done')\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
